## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — Severity-Ranked Findings

This test suite is a textbook example of **"coverage without verification"** — every production method is invoked (achieving line coverage) but almost nothing meaningful is asserted.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests That Prove Nothing

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Bug It Misses |
|---|------|-------------|---------------|
| 1 | `AddProduct_Works` (L17-21) | **No assertion at all.** Only calls `AddProduct`. | Could silently discard the product. |
| 2 | `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33-36) | **No assertion.** Calls `UpdatePrice` but never reads back. | Price could remain unchanged; wrong product could be updated. |
| 3 | `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39-43) | **No assertion.** Never checks the product is actually gone. | Delete could be a no-op. |
| 4 | `GetProductCount_Works` (L63-66) | **No assertion.** Doesn't even add products first. | Could return -1 and the test passes. |
| 5 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L68-74) | **No assertion.** Never reads prices after discount. | Discount math (`Price * (1 - fraction)`) could be inverted or zeroed. |
| 6 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L84-88) | **No assertion.** Never verifies products are persisted. | Parsing errors or data corruption would go unnoticed. |

**Fix pattern:** Add `Assert.AreEqual` on the expected state after the action:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
    Assert.AreEqual(product, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}

[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
}

[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_DeletesProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}
```

---

### 🟠 High — Weak Assertions (Assert.IsNotNull on non-nullable paths)

| # | Test | Problem |
|---|------|---------|
| 7 | `GetProduct_Works` (L25-29) | Asserts `IsNotNull` — never checks the returned values (`Sku`, `Name`, `Price`). Could return a corrupted product. |
| 8 | `SearchProducts_Works` (L47-52) | `IsNotNull` on a `List<>` (always non-null). Never checks count or content. Could return 0 results or wrong products. |
| 9 | `GetAllProducts_Works` (L55-60) | Same — `IsNotNull` on a list. Never checks it contains the added product. |
| 10 | `ExportToCsv_Works` (L77-82) | `IsNotNull` on a `string` that's always non-null from `string.Join`. Never validates CSV format. |

**Fix pattern:** Assert on content, not just existence:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Search_ReturnsMatchingProducts()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-002", "Gadget", 19.99m));
    var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
    Assert.AreEqual(1, results.Count);
    Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", results[0].Sku);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ExportToCsv_FormatsCorrectly()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    var csv = _catalog.ExportToCsv();
    Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001,Widget,9.99", csv);
}
